  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and I read the rule back after enabling it. It shows as enabled.\n\n- **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" uses `timestamp` as its time field.\n- **Dashboard:** \"S4-6a43fcfd Web logs\" has two panels. One is \"Requests over time\" and the other is the response-code panel.\n- **Alert rule:** \"S4-6a43fcfd 5xx errors\" runs every minute. It fires when more than 10 requests with a response code from 500 to 599 arrive within 5 minutes. It was created disabled, so I enabled it. Its status reads \"ok\".\n- **Panel edit:** The response-code panel is now titled \"Responses by code\". The dashboard read-back confirms the new title.\n\nThe rule matches codes with a text range from \"500\" to \"599\" on `response.keyword`, because `response` is stored as a string. That covers every three-digit code in that span. I didn't run the query against the data, so I haven't confirmed how many requests it matches.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
